I’ll inspect the specimen metadata, diffraction grid, and the frozen analysis utilities first. Then I’ll build the simplest chemically plausible multiphase model, refine it in GSAS-II, and verify that every coherent residual family is accounted for before writing the deliverables.

The metadata points to a low-temperature CaCO₃ + WO₃ reaction, so the first complete inventory to test is CaWO₄ plus plausible residual CaCO₃/WO₃ (and CaO only if demanded). I’m now querying the frozen COD index for exact compositions and comparing full reflection families, not just the strongest peaks.

The observed reflection families support three ordinary phases already: monoclinic WO₃ (the 23–25° triplet and its 33–35° family), scheelite CaWO₄ (18.9°, 29.0°, and higher-angle families), and calcite CaCO₃ (29.7°, 39.7°, 43.46°, 48.78°, etc.). I’m moving these into a replayable three-phase GSAS-II fit and will compare the physically valid same-composition WO₃ references at that checkpoint.

The first complete model is stable and gives preliminary crystalline fractions of roughly 56% WO₃, 37% CaCO₃, and 6–7% CaWO₄. Peak positions improve strongly when all three cells are refined. The angular zero and specimen displacement are nearly collinear, so I’ll retain only a physically anchored calibration combination in the final model rather than over-interpreting both separately.

A bounded-coordinate trial exposed a GSAS-II convention issue: coordinate limits apply to the absolute fractional coordinate even though the refined variable is named as a displacement. That branch drove atoms to artificial near-zero coordinates, so I stopped it and am restarting from the intact pre-coordinate checkpoint with bounds centered on each published site. No bad-geometry branch will enter the final project.

The constant offset/height decomposition can be anchored from the data grid itself: its lower bound is displaced by exactly +0.087° from the frozen laboratory grid. I’m fixing that independently observed constant and refining only specimen height; this removes the >99.9% zero/height correlation while preserving the physically relevant angular correction.

All coherent reflections remain assigned to the three accepted phases; the remaining residuals are derivative-shaped at those same peak positions, so I’m stopping phase/library search. The anchored physical model is at Rwp ≈ 7.64%. I’ll now test only bounded specimen/profile terms and texture axes supported by those residuals, then keep the simplest improving branch.

I found an important reproducibility check: a fresh GSAS calculation after one branch did not exactly reproduce that branch’s cached curve. Since the required project is scored by regeneration, I’m validating candidate checkpoints with a fresh atomic replay and will submit only a checkpoint whose replay—not its cached array—has the reported fit.

I’ve isolated the reliable checkpoint: a physical three-phase refinement whose full-grid replay reproduces Rwp 7.3253% with no structural review flags. I’m now packaging that exact checkpoint as `final.gpx`, regenerating all submitted profiles/CIFs from it, and running a final consistency check so none of the exploratory branches leak into the deliverables.

The exact replay confirms all 8,293 points, all three positive phase scales, and no negative displacement parameters or other structural flags. The accepted model is scheelite CaWO₄, calcite CaCO₃, and monoclinic P2₁/c WO₃; the exploratory surface-correction branch is being discarded because it did not reproduce its cached fit on replay.

Packaging is complete. I’ve kept the reported fractions on the crystalline-only basis and explicitly separated the very small covariance errors from the larger systematic uncertainty caused by overlap, preferred orientation, and missing pointwise uncertainties. One final validation remains: parse every CIF/result field and replay `final.gpx` against the submitted `fit.csv`.

Final validation passed: `result.json` parses as a complete three-phase normalized inventory, each submitted CIF maps uniquely to its corresponding `final.gpx` phase (including ADPs/coordinates), the project replay has no physical review flags, and `fit.csv` covers all 8,293 original points with independently recomputed Rwp 7.325327%.

Analysis complete. The crystalline phase fractions are:

- Monoclinic WO₃ (P2₁/c): 56.06 wt%
- Calcite CaCO₃ (R-3c): 31.38 wt%
- Scheelite CaWO₄ (I4₁/a): 12.56 wt%

Full-grid GSAS-II replay: Rwp = 7.3253%, Rp = 5.3795%. No coherent fourth-phase reflection family remained; trace crystalline or amorphous material cannot be excluded.

